# Analysing a Castor EDC study with Dux

```elixir
# The checkout this notebook is in, with ex_castor_edc and ex_castor_edc_kino
# checked out next to it (or where CASTOR_EDC_PATH and CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH
# say). Outside a checkout, use {:ex_castor_edc_dux, "~> 0.1.0"} instead.
castor_edc = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_PATH", Path.expand("../../ex_castor_edc", __DIR__))
kino = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH", Path.expand("../../ex_castor_edc_kino", __DIR__))

Mix.install([
  {:ex_castor_edc_dux, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)},
  {:ex_castor_edc_kino, path: kino},
  {:ex_castor_edc, path: castor_edc, override: true},
  {:kino_dux, "~> 0.2.0"}
])
```

## Introduction

This notebook loads a study's data into Dux frames, backed by DuckDB, and takes a first look at it.
The data goes to Parquet files as it streams in from the API, so it never has to fit in memory, and every frame's column types come from the study's structure.

## Connecting

Add your API credentials as Livebook secrets: `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID` and `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET`, which you can create on your [account settings page](https://data.castoredc.com/account/settings).
The connection cell below reads them from there, so they never appear in the notebook: pick the server your studies are on, and test the connection.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.ConnectionCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
client =
  CastorEDC.new(
    client_id: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID"),
    client_secret: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET"),
    base_url: "https://data.castoredc.com"
  )
```

Then pick the study to look at; the cell lists the studies the client can reach.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJjbGllbnQiOiJjbGllbnQiLCJzdHVkeV9pZCI6IiIsInN0dWR5X25hbWUiOiIiLCJ2YXJpYWJsZSI6InN0dWR5X2lkIn0","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.StudyCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

The frames' files go in one directory; remove it when you're done.

```elixir
dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "castor_edc_#{study_id}")
```

## The study's structure

`fields/3` has a row per field that holds values, in the order the study shows them: where it collects data, its type, and its option group.
`option_groups/3` has a row per option, to join on `option_group_id`.

```elixir
{:ok, fields} = CastorEDC.Dux.fields(client, study_id, dir: dir)
{:ok, options} = CastorEDC.Dux.option_groups(client, study_id, dir: dir)

fields
|> Dux.compute()
```

## Participants and sites

```elixir
{:ok, participants} = CastorEDC.Dux.participants(client, study_id, dir: dir)
{:ok, sites} = CastorEDC.Dux.sites(client, study_id, dir: dir)

participants
|> Dux.join(Dux.select(sites, [:site_id, :name]), on: :site_id, how: :left)
|> Dux.group_by(:name)
|> Dux.summarise_with(participants: "COUNT(participant_id)", mean_progress: "AVG(progress)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

## Collected data

`participant_data/3` runs a batch export and queries its CSV files directly: a frame for the study's values, with a row per participant, and one for each repeating data and survey, with a row per instance.
It takes a few requests, however big the study is.

```elixir
{:ok, %{study: study, repeating_data: repeating_data, surveys: surveys}} =
  CastorEDC.Dux.participant_data(client, study_id, dir: dir, missing_columns: true)

study
|> Dux.compute()
```

Values marked missing are nulls, with their codes (95 to 99) in the `<variable>_missing` columns.

```elixir
Map.keys(repeating_data) ++ Map.keys(surveys)
```

`data_points/3` reads the same values through the API instead, page by page, with a row per value, which suits filtering and pivoting:

```elixir
{:ok, values} = CastorEDC.Dux.data_points(client, study_id, dir: dir)

values
|> Dux.filter_with("missing IS NOT NULL")
|> Dux.group_by([:variable_name, :missing])
|> Dux.summarise_with(count: "COUNT(*)")
|> Dux.compute()
```

With `format: :wide`, it gives the same shape as `participant_data/3`, with a column per variable.
